# Next work: VM labs, container capsules, and local factories

Treat this as one infrastructure program with three connected designs. Each lab is a VM with its own evolving AI4Research instance, project resources, capability factory, local Docker images and run history. Labs start from a tested AI4R base and can contribute selected CCs and platform changes back. The worktree analogy describes independent development histories; VM runtime state, resources and images also require manifests and backups beyond Git.

**Direction:** full CC containerization is the design target. The first conversion proves the implementation route. Expected small overhead relative to capsule runtime is the user's working assumption, not a measured result; measurements guide capacity and implementation choices.

**Status:** roadmap and initial architecture briefs, with no committed dates. The maintained M1 architecture remains authoritative for current implementation; proposed departures need native reconciliation. No VM/factory deployment or new performance result is claimed.

## Three connected design deliverables

| Design | Responsibility | Concrete design exit |
|---|---|---|
| [[Designs/VM Lab Infrastructure Design|VM lab infrastructure]] | VM lifecycle, identity, project resources, local images, model/compute access, recovery and exchange | Topology, lab manifest, resource/permission matrix, backup/restore and central contribution boundaries |
| [[Designs/Container Capability Capsule Design|Container CCs]] | Executable package, invocation, checks, pinned dependencies and evidence | Package/invocation examples, binder-runner-check ownership, failure behavior and conversion path |
| [[Designs/Per Lab Capability Factory Design|Per-lab factory]] | Independent authoring/build/qualification inside every AI4R | Source-to-image journey, job recovery, local eligibility and separate central adoption |

## Work order

| Order | Work | Exit |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | Establish shared terms and one worked journey | Define lab, tested base, CC version, image, invocation, factory attempt and promotion; assign each boundary an owner |
| 2 | Draft VM topology and CC contract together | Locate AI4R, factory, builder, runner, checks, images and durable data; walk one invocation across boundaries |
| 3 | Complete factory and contribution design | Specify build inputs, candidate package, local admission, central rebuild/requalification, conflicts and rollback |
| 4 | Reconcile with maintained M1 | Map dispatch, prepared-source policy, research-plan shape, composition and manual admission to explicit proposed changes; preserve native TASK/AC/IF identities |
| 5 | Prove one vertical slice | Two real VMs from one base; independent factories/images; a CC built in A and independently qualified in B; interruption, export and restore evidence |
| 6 | Expand conversion and contribution | Convert remaining CC classes; demonstrate separate CC and platform promotions, compatible new release pins and rollback |

Steps 1–3 are the next design work. Detailed drafting can overlap once shared terms are fixed. CC accuracy work continues alongside this program; infrastructure acceptance does not prove an accuracy gain. Detailed schemas and implementation tasks belong in the native specification process.

## Shared identity model

A **tested base release** records AI4R source, deployment definition, compatible CC/declaration/check pins, factory version, state migrations and qualification evidence. Master means this well-tested role; no branch name or floating image tag is assumed. Git holds source history; image storage holds executable artifacts; admission records hold eligibility; artifact storage holds resources and evidence.

A **lab manifest** records lab/project identity, parent base release, current AI4R/factory revisions and local deltas, VM/runtime platform, catalog/image pins, resource allocations and durable state/evidence locations. Evolution creates a new revision. Each invocation records what it actually used.

A **candidate** records parent lineage, exact source tree including uncommitted deltas, build inputs, image digest/platform, declaration/check pins and evaluation evidence. Image identity captures packaged software; external data, models, services and hardware remain explicit dependencies.

## The worked journey for all three designs

1. Provision A and B from base R0 with factory F0 and a pinned CC catalog. A works on project P; B works on Q.
2. A's factory consumes an authorized prepared source snapshot and builds candidate C1 locally, recording source, recipe, environment and image pins.
3. Independent local qualification may make C1 eligible for a new plan in A. Already-frozen runs retain their pins. B continues independently.
4. A also changes its planner. Record platform candidate P1 separately, with any compatibility dependency on C1.
5. Export C1 source/build inputs and evidence. Central qualification rebuilds and tests the exact artifact proposed for release. A different rebuild digest is a distinct artifact requiring its own qualification and provenance link.
6. Adopt compatible sources, images and admission records as tested base R1. P1 has its own release path; if C1 depends on it, qualify their compatibility set explicitly.
7. B can retain R0, selectively import a compatible C1, or explicitly rebase to R1. Requalify affected behavior, resolve conflicts and record state migrations. Rollback restores a compatible release/state pair and retains failure evidence.

## Direction changes and current contracts

The earlier optional/selective carrier experiment is superseded by full containerization as the requested target. A one-CC pilot still bounds the first implementation. A lab is a VM; the existing single-host container manager is bounded evidence and possible reusable tooling. The factory is local to each AI4R with independent state/versions/images; shared registries support exchange rather than providing a mandatory central factory.

AI4R itself may evolve locally. Ordinary platform code need not become a CC. Local eligibility and selected central adoption remain distinct. Current M1 protected gates, output-only semantic verifier, frozen runs and manual activation are preserved until their owning contracts change. Earlier single-host Docker smoke evidence remains valid within its synthetic scope.

## Design readiness

- [ ] All three designs share identities and the worked journey.
- [ ] Provision, build, run, check, locally admit, export, centrally qualify, update and restore have explicit owners.
- [ ] Missing image, incompatible platform/GPU, unavailable model, cancellation, storage failure and interrupted build/run have outcomes.
- [ ] M1 departures and affected task/interface owners are recorded without invented approvals or PASS claims.
- [ ] The trial defines evidence for two VMs, factory independence, traceability, recovery and selective promotion.

Deployment backend, resource sizing, catalog serialization, model route, release custodian and first representative capsule remain design choices. They do not block shared-contract drafting. The batch source receipt records the bounded owner reading; this is not a full PRD/code audit.
